# phasekv — phase-aware, query-adaptive KV-cache compression

An optimized successor design to the presses in
[NVIDIA/kvpress](https://github.com/NVIDIA/kvpress) (1,240 stars,
191 forks, Apache-2.0, checked 2026-10-29), built and benchmarked
without running kvpress itself: baseline presses are re-implemented
from their published scoring rules and compared against the new
policy on a shared simulated attention testbed, on identical
contexts, budgets, and seeds. **All benchmark numbers below are
simulation numbers from that testbed, not measurements of kvpress
on real models.** See [docs/RESEARCH.md](docs/RESEARCH.md) for the
trending check, sources, and rejected alternatives, and
[docs/ADAPTING_TO_KVPRESS.md](docs/ADAPTING_TO_KVPRESS.md) for how
the design maps onto real kvpress presses (untested on real models).

## The weakness being optimized

kvpress presses score KV pairs from the observation window and apply
one keep fraction everywhere. Three things go wrong in the
shared-context reuse setting — compression happens before the
question is known, and the cache is reused for later questions:

1. **Query blindness.** A press that scores from the first question's
   context keeps facts the first question needed and drops facts a
   later question needs. SnapKV-style scoring cannot recover them.
2. **Phase weak spots.** Chunked compression adds a phase
   coordinate (position relative to window boundaries). Zhu et al.
   (arXiv:2609.36322) report retrieval accuracy differing by up to
   40 percentage points across phases in real models; average
   benchmarks conceal it.
3. **Irreversible eviction.** Nothing verifies that the retained
   set can still answer probe queries, or restores within budget
   when it cannot.

## What phasekv does differently

The `PhaseAdaptivePolicy` makes three changes, each ablatable:

1. **Per-head adaptive budgets.** Budget is allocated by measured
   head behaviour (attention concentration in the observation
   window), floored so no head is starved, instead of one uniform
   keep fraction per head.
2. **Phase-stratified retention.** Within each head, the budget is
   split across compression phases (stride 4 by default), so no
   phase class is starved.
3. **Verified-recall rescue swaps.** A set of representative probe
   queries (from a query log in deployment; a held-out query set
   here, never the eval questions) measures retained attention
   mass. If it is below threshold, the policy swaps in the
   highest probe-scored evicted pairs for the lowest kept pairs —
   at most 10% of budget per head — so the total never grows.
   An optional **merge-downgrade** folds evicted values into their
   nearest kept neighbour (averaged, preserving value scale)
   instead of deleting them outright.

Original work and attribution: the baseline scoring rules are the
published rules behind kvpress presses (SnapKV, Knorm, Expected
Attention, StreamingLLM, TOVA, chunked pooling, random). The
phase-adaptive policy, the simulated testbed, and all code in this
repo are original implementations written for this project. No
kvpress code is included. License is Apache-2.0, matching kvpress.

## Benchmark: original-style presses vs phasekv

Workload: simulated 8-head attention, 512 tokens, 16 planted needle
facts in two groups. Group A is what observation-window queries
point at; group B is what later questions point at. Keep fraction
0.25 (128 of 512 positions per head), 5 seeds. Metrics use eval
queries only, which no policy sees. Full grid (512/1024 tokens ×
keep 0.5/0.25/0.125 × 5 seeds) is in `results/experiment.json`;
headline aggregates in `results/measured.json`.

| Policy | Needle recall | Worst-phase recall | Mass retained | Output rel. error |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Random | 0.225 | 0.163 | 0.250 | 1.728 |
| StreamingLLM | 0.250 | 0.000 | 0.250 | 1.664 |
| SnapKV | 0.445 | 0.381 | 0.252 | 1.671 |
| Knorm | 0.195 | 0.125 | 0.250 | 1.748 |
| Expected attention | 0.445 | 0.381 | 0.252 | 1.671 |
| Chunked (block top-1) | 0.441 | 0.338 | 0.258 | 1.638 |
| TOVA | 0.434 | 0.344 | 0.252 | 1.698 |
| **phasekv (this repo)** | **0.591** | **0.525** | 0.254 | 1.710 |
| phasekv, no rescue (ablation) | 0.445 | 0.394 | 0.252 | 1.675 |

At the same budget, phasekv recalls 0.591 vs 0.445 for SnapKV
(+0.146) and lifts worst-phase recall from 0.381 to 0.525 (+0.144).
The gain is attributable to rescue: the no-rescue ablation ties
SnapKV (0.445), while group-B recall alone goes from 0.300
(SnapKV) to 0.659 (phasekv) — at the cost of some group-A recall
(0.591 → 0.522), the expected trade for a cache compressed before
the question is known. The advantage holds at other budgets
(keep 0.5: 0.720 vs 0.636; keep 0.125: 0.489 vs 0.333) and at 1024
tokens (0.600 vs 0.452). Cache size is identical at matched keep
fractions (131,072 bytes at the headline cell for every policy
except chunked, 134,144, whose block-plus-sink rule keeps slightly
more). With merge-downgrade enabled, output error at the headline
cell falls from 1.710 to 0.609 with recall unchanged — merging
preserves value information that eviction deletes.

StreamingLLM's worst-phase recall of 0.000 is the phase weak spot
made visible: a recency-only cache keeps nothing from early phases.

## Quickstart

```bash
python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt -r requirements-dev.txt
pip install -e .

# Compare all policies on one context
PYTHONPATH=src python -m phasekv.cli demo

# Score one policy on one context as JSON
PYTHONPATH=src python -m phasekv.cli audit --policy phase_adaptive --keep 0.25

# Benchmark table for one cell
PYTHONPATH=src python scripts/benchmark.py

# Full grid (writes results/experiment.json and results/measured.json)
PYTHONPATH=src python scripts/run_experiment.py

# Tests
python -m pytest -q
```

Example policy config: `examples/policy.yaml`. Example context
parameters: `examples/context.json`.

## Limitations, stated honestly

- **Simulated testbed, not real models.** Keys, values, and queries
  are synthetic with planted structure. Relative policy behaviour
  under controlled conditions is what is measured; nothing here
  proves the same gains on a real transformer. GPU evaluation
  against real kvpress presses is the required follow-up.
- **Unit-norm keys.** Keys and queries are normalised so
  discrimination is directional. This deliberately weakens the
  Knorm rule (its signal is key norm); on real models Knorm's
  behaviour will differ.
- **Probe queries are a design input.** Rescue needs representative
  probe queries. If the probe set does not resemble later
  questions, rescue cannot help, and using eval questions as
  probes would leak the test set — the code keeps probe and eval
  query streams separate by construction.
- **Merge-downgrade is value-only.** Evicted keys are dropped;
  only value information is folded into neighbours. It improves
  output fidelity in simulation but changes the cache semantics
  and needs real-model validation.
- **Chunked baseline is strong.** The chunked policy keeps the
  best-scored token per block; pooled chunking (one entry per
  block) would be weaker and more phase-sensitive, so the
  chunked numbers here are a generous baseline.
- **In-process, single testbed.** No serving, batching, or
  multi-request lifecycle; SCBench-style lifecycle evaluation is
  out of scope.

